course: history
lesson: 54
title: China: the Silk Road and the Tang
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Mei says China built the Silk Road and only silk went along it. The teacher calls it «a road». Both miss something: it was a web of land and sea routes with China at one end, and merchants of many peoples, like the Sogdians, carried goods both ways.
explain: Think of a school swap shop: you bring stickers and go home with a ball. Silk, paper, porcelain and tea went west; horses, glass, grapes and cotton came east, and so did ideas, like Buddhism from India.
explain: Under the Tang dynasty, from 618 to 907, the capital Chang'an was one of the greatest cities on Earth, full of traders, poets and travellers. The monk Xuanzang walked all the way to India for holy books, and a later novel turned his trip into the adventures of the Monkey King.
explain: Golden ages end too: the An Lushan rebellion of 755 shook the Tang so hard that it never fully recovered.
fact: The Silk Road was a network of land and sea trade routes linking China with Central Asia, India, Persia, the Middle East and Europe, from the Han period onward.
fact: The name «Silk Road» was made up by a German geographer in 1877, and no one country built it; merchants of many peoples, such as the Sogdians, traders from Central Asia, carried the goods.
fact: Silk, paper, porcelain and tea went west; horses, glass, grapes, cotton and new religions came east, and ideas and diseases travelled too.
fact: Buddhism began in India and reached China around the 1st century AD; it became one of China's great traditions, and Chinese forms spread to Korea and Japan.
fact: The monk Xuanzang walked to India in the 7th century and brought back Buddhist texts; centuries later the novel «Journey to the West» turned his trip into an adventure with the Monkey King.
fact: The Tang dynasty, 618–907, was a golden age; its capital Chang'an was among the largest cities in the world, though Baghdad was also huge.
fact: Li Bai and Du Fu were the most famous Tang poets, and children in China learn their poems by heart; Li Bai's «Quiet Night Thought» describes moonlight that makes him miss home.
fact: A legend says Li Bai drowned reaching for the Moon's reflection, but he probably died of illness.
fact: Wu Zetian ruled as emperor in her own name from 690 to 705, the only woman to do so; later, Empress Dowager Cixi held great power without the title.
fact: The An Lushan rebellion, 755–763, devastated the Tang; the counted population fell sharply, and the dynasty never fully recovered.
fact: In 751 Abbasid (Arab Muslim) forces beat a Tang army near the Talas river; a later story says Chinese prisoners taught paper-making in Samarkand, but historians think paper probably reached Central Asia earlier.
fact: Go (weiqi) and xiangqi (Chinese chess) are old Chinese games, Go over 2,500 years old; modern chess grew from the Indian game chaturanga.
fact: Chopsticks have been used in China for at least 3,000 years and spread to Japan, Korea and Vietnam; in much of Asia, like India and Thailand, people traditionally eat with hands or spoons and forks.
exercise: What was the Silk Road? | A web of land and sea trade routes | One straight paved road from Beijing to Rome | A river in China | A wall made of silk
exercise: When was the name «Silk Road» made up? | In 1877, by a German geographer | In 221 BC, by the First Emperor | In 1405, by Zheng He | In 1950, by a film maker
exercise: Who carried many goods along the Silk Road? | Merchants of many peoples, like the Sogdians | Only Chinese soldiers guarding the emperor's silk | Only the Romans | Robots
exercise: Which of these came east into China? | Horses, glass, grapes and cotton | Paper, porcelain, silk and tea leaves | Gunpowder and silk | Pandas and bamboo
exercise: Which religion came into China along the Silk Road? | Buddhism | Japanese Shinto | Chinese Daoism | Confucianism
exercise: Where did Buddhism begin? | India | China itself | Southern Japan | Ancient Greece
exercise: What did Xuanzang bring back from India? | Buddhist texts | A giraffe | Spanish silver | Gunpowder
exercise: Which novel turned Xuanzang's trip into an adventure? | Journey to the West | The Art of War | The Dao De Jing | Robinson Crusoe
exercise: When did the Tang dynasty rule? | 618–907 | 221–206 BC | 1368–1644 | 1644–1912
exercise: What was the Tang capital called? | Chang'an | Hangzhou, by the West Lake | Nanjing on the Yangtze | Kyoto in Japan
exercise: Who were the most famous Tang poets? | Li Bai and Du Fu | Confucius and Laozi | Sun Tzu and Cai Lun | Marco Polo and Ricci
exercise: What does «Quiet Night Thought» describe? | Moonlight that makes the poet miss home | A battle fought at night by the light of torches | A dragon flying | A storm at sea
exercise: What was special about Wu Zetian? | She ruled as emperor in her own name | She built the Great Wall to keep out the Mongols | She sailed to Africa | She invented paper
exercise: What happened after the An Lushan rebellion? | The counted population fell sharply and the Tang never fully recovered | The Tang grew richer than ever and ruled for another thousand years in peace | China joined Rome | Nothing changed
exercise: Who won the battle near the Talas river in 751? | The Abbasids | The Tang | The Mongols | The Romans
exercise: Which board game is Chinese without doubt? | Go | Chess | Draughts | Snakes and ladders
exercise: Where did modern chess come from? | The Indian game chaturanga | Chinese Go | Ancient Egypt | England in 1863
exercise: Which countries traditionally use chopsticks? | China, Japan, Korea and Vietnam | India, Thailand and Indonesia | All of Asia without a single exception | Only Japan
test: Mei says «China built the Silk Road», the teacher says «it was a road». What is wiser? | A web of routes, with China at one end and many peoples trading | One long stone road that China built alone across the deserts to Rome | One road the Romans built | A railway
test: Mei says only silk went west. What would she find in the merchants' bags? | Paper, porcelain and tea too, and goods coming back east | Only silk, nothing else at all, packed in bales on the camels | Only gold | Only pandas
test: The teacher says Buddhism is Indian, Mei says Chinese. Who is right? | Both: Indian in origin, Chinese in many later forms | Only the teacher | Only Mei, because it first began in China under the Tang | Neither; it began in Rome
test: lol mei swears the monkey king walked to india for real. did he? | The monk Xuanzang did; the Monkey King is from a novel | Yes, the Monkey King was real, and his footprints are still in India | No, nobody went to India | The Monkey King was an emperor
test: Mei says the Tang was the richest country on Earth. What is safer to say? | One of the great powers; Chang'an was among the largest cities | The only rich country ever | A small poor village | Richer than all the other countries of the world put together, by far
test: Mei says Li Bai drowned hugging the Moon. What should she add? | It is a legend; he probably died of illness | It is proven by police reports from the river | He lived on the Moon | He never wrote poems
test: If diseases also travelled along the Silk Road, what does that tell us? | Trade routes carry germs as well as goods | Germs cannot travel | Only silk can travel | Traders never got ill
test: Mei says Wu Zetian was the only woman who ruled China. What is the exact version? | The only woman to rule as emperor in her own name | Many women had that title, both before and after her time | No woman ever had power | Cixi had the same title
test: The teacher tells the Talas prisoners story as fact. Mei read some doubts. What is wise? | Tell it as a story; paper probably arrived earlier | It is proven beyond doubt | It never happened, there was no battle | Paper came from Samarkand to China
test: «everyone in asia eats with chopsticks» says Mei. who'd correct her? | Someone from Thailand or India | Nobody; she is right | A Roman | A penguin
test: Mei claims China invented chess. What should she proudly claim instead? | Go, which is clearly Chinese | Football | Snakes and ladders | Cards
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the Silk Road? | A network of overland and maritime routes, traversed by merchants of numerous peoples | A single paved highway constructed by the Tang | A canal linking Beijing with Rome | A route reserved exclusively for the trade in silk and forbidden to all other merchandise
exam: Identify the religion that, originating in India, was transmitted into China by way of the Silk Road. | Buddhism | Philosophical Daoism | Confucianism | The Shinto of Japan
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning Wu Zetian. | She alone among women ruled as emperor in her own name | She held power without the imperial title | She founded the Han dynasty | She commanded the voyages to Africa
exam: Ascertain the consequence for the Tang dynasty of the rebellion commenced by An Lushan in 755. | A grave decline from which the dynasty never fully recovered | An immediate and lasting increase in the prosperity of the empire | The unification of China under the Qin | The transfer of the capital to Rome
